event: response.function_call_arguments.done
data: {"type":"response.function_call_arguments.done","sequence_number":1346,"item_id":"fc_062e8dfcc6e93aa1016adb6974a0508191a76cdf5d57e2ccf5","output_index":2,"arguments":"{\"artifact\":{\"inference\":\"R004\",\"packet_sha256\":\"d208f31847e08993aa9407267a603fabd46be4e5231df6af97f052df36f43fb8\",\"verdict\":\"FAIL\",\"notes\":\"Read boundary: the complete packet.json (lines 1–502) and the declared Yui Audit A, self-containment, and NL0-format rules. I treat the listed inputs as conditional premises; I did not inspect source proofs or certify carrier identity, isolation, runtime execution, or source fidelity. The first unsupported implication is the assertion that joint smoothness gives the equality of mixed partial derivatives ∂t∂x_j u=∂x_j u_t. This is the substantive equality-of-mixed-partials result; neither an input nor a complete local proof of it is supplied. The permitted finite-dimensional differentiation/inner-product calculation policy does not import that theorem. Consequently the displayed integration-by-parts identity does not establish the claimed derivative formula from this packet alone. Packet replay verdict is this mathematical conditional, not an execution-isolation verdict.\",\"checks\":{\"inputs\":{\"verdict\":\"PASS\",\"notes\":\"N001 and N002 are listed direct premises, and M001, M011, M017 are the listed theorem inputs. Their stated applications have identifiable bindings and the needed hypotheses are addressed in the packet. This is conditional acceptance of the packet's input propositions, not source-fidelity verification.\",\"locations\":[\"/inputs\",\"/inference/inputs\",\"/inference/applications\"]},\"argument\":{\"verdict\":\"FAIL\",\"notes\":\"The first unsupported implication occurs in S001: joint smoothness is said to give ∂t∂x_j u=∂x_j u_t. Equality of these mixed partials is not a finite-dimensional differentiation identity supplied as a fixed calculation here, and no local derivation or direct theorem input establishes it. The subsequent differentiation of the gradient-square term therefore cannot be identified with the term used in integration by parts on this packet alone.\",\"locations\":[\"/inference/argument/0/text\",\"/logic/calculations\",\"/inference/inputs\"]},\"dependency_accounting\":{\"verdict\":\"FAIL\",\"notes\":\"The argument uses the equality-of-mixed-partials theorem without listing it as an input and without proving it locally. The blanket calculation policy only permits finite-dimensional differentiation and inner-product identities; it cannot import this substantive theorem. The other listed inputs have identifiable roles, but this unlisted result blocks dependency closure.\",\"locations\":[\"/inference/argument/0/text\",\"/logic/calculations\",\"/inference/inputs\"]},\"output\":{\"verdict\":\"PASS\",\"notes\":\"N003 states the derivative identity for every positive r, including the integral, factor 2, and wave-operator expression. S001 fixes arbitrary r and purports to derive precisely that statement; the failure is in the argument, not an identified mismatch in the output formulation.\",\"locations\":[\"/output/statement\",\"/inference/argument/0/text\"]},\"scope\":{\"verdict\":\"PASS\",\"notes\":\"The argument fixes arbitrary r>0 and invokes N001 for a time window with r in its interior. Its coordinate-index argument is explicitly intended for arbitrary j followed by summation over the finite index set. No scope escape is needed for the identified failure.\",\"locations\":[\"/inference/locals/0\",\"/inference/locals/1\",\"/inference/argument/0/text\"]},\"applicability\":{\"verdict\":\"PASS\",\"notes\":\"The M001 application specifies the Euclidean/Borel/Lebesgue space, scalar codomain, finite-on-compacts fact, and continuity/compact-support grounds. M011's application gives the vector spaces, dot-product bilinear map, integrability obligations, derivative identities, and relevant supports; M017 is applied to the finite index coordinate realization to supply the Haar-measure fact. These application checks do not prove the separate mixed-partials implication.\",\"locations\":[\"/inference/applications/0\",\"/inference/applications/1\",\"/inference/applications/2\",\"/inputs/2/source_contract\",\"/inputs/3/source_contract\",\"/inputs/4/source_contract\"]},\"statement_closure\":{\"verdict\":\"PASS\",\"notes\":\"The direct input and output statements give the substantive integrability, support, differentiability, and energy-derivative claims used here; the output gives the full claimed formula. No absent formula or condition in the statements is the cause of failure.\",\"locations\":[\"/inputs/0/statement\",\"/inputs/1/statement\",\"/output/statement\"]},\"object_closure\":{\"verdict\":\"PASS\",\"notes\":\"The principal objects used in S001 have declarations in the parameters, definitions, input statements, or R004 locals: r, j, v_j, f_j, g, u, E, and Δ_x. Their uses are in the stated scope. The mixed-partials defect is a missing factual justification, not an undeclared-object defect.\",\"locations\":[\"/parameters\",\"/definitions\",\"/inference/locals\",\"/inference/argument/0/text\"]},\"dependency_closure\":{\"verdict\":\"FAIL\",\"notes\":\"Reverse-checking the actual prose reveals an ungrounded factual use: joint smoothness is used to infer equality of mixed partials. That fact is neither a direct input nor a prior local fact, and the one-step argument does not prove it. Thus the argument is not closed over the listed inputs.\",\"locations\":[\"/inference/argument/0/text\",\"/inference/inputs\",\"/logic/calculations\"]},\"packet_replay\":{\"verdict\":\"FAIL\",\"notes\":\"On the packet alone and conditional on its listed input propositions, the proof does not suffice: the first unsupported implication is joint smoothness ⇒ ∂t∂x_j u=∂x_j u_t. I read the packet and the relevant declared rules in this context. This judgment does not certify execution isolation or runtime attestation; those are outside this mathematical replay.\",\"locations\":[\"/inference/argument/0/text\",\"/logic/calculations\",\"/inputs\"]}},\"findings\":[{\"severity\":\"blocking\",\"location\":\"/inference/argument/0/text\",\"premises\":\"u is jointly smooth; the stated calculation policy permits finite-dimensional differentiation and inner-product identities.\",\"conclusion\":\"∂t∂x_j u=∂x_j u_t.\",\"why_unsupported\":\"The inference is equality of mixed partial derivatives, a substantive theorem rather than merely differentiating a displayed expression. The packet provides no direct theorem input or local proof of that result, and the author-written calculation policy cannot add it as a premise.\"}]}}"}

