the complete packet.json (lines 1–502) and the declared Yui Audit A, self-containment, and NL0-format rules. I treat the listed inputs as conditional premises; I did not inspect source proofs or certify carrier identity, isolation, runtime execution, or source fidelity. The first unsupported implication is the assertion that joint smoothness gives the equality of mixed partial derivatives ∂t∂x_j u=∂x_j u_t. This is the substantive equality-of-mixed-partials result; neither an input nor a complete local proof of it is supplied. The permitted finite-dimensional differentiation/inner-product calculation policy does not import that theorem. Consequently the displayed integration-by-parts identity does not establish the claimed derivative formula from this packet alone. Packet replay verdict is this mathematical conditional, not an execution-isolation verdict.\",\"checks\":{\"inputs\":{\"verdict\":\"PASS\",\"notes\":\"N001 and N002 are listed direct premises, and M001, M011, M017 are the listed theorem inputs. Their stated applications have identifiable bindings and the needed hypotheses are addressed in the packet. This is conditional acceptance of the packet's input propositions, not source-fidelity verification.\",\"locations\":[\"/inputs\",\"/inference/inputs\",\"/inference/applications\"]},\"argument\":{\"verdict\":\"FAIL\",\"notes\":\"The first unsupported implication occurs in S001: joint smoothness is said to give ∂t∂x_j u=∂x_j u_t. Equality of these mixed partials is not a finite-dimensional differentiation identity supplied as a fixed calculation here, and no local derivation or direct theorem input establishes it. The subsequent differentiation of the gradient-square term therefore cannot be identified with the term used in integration by parts on this packet alone.\",\"locations\":[\"/inference/argument/0/text\",\"/logic/calculations\",\"/inference/inputs\"]},\"dependency_accounting\":{\"verdict\":\"FAIL\",\"notes\":\"The argument uses the equality-of-mixed-partials theorem without listing it as an input and without proving it locally. The blanket calculation policy only permits finite-dimensional differentiation and inner-product identities; it cannot import this substantive theorem. The other listed inputs have identifiable roles, but this unlisted result blocks dependency closure.\",\"locations\":[\"/inference/argument/0/text\",\"/logic/calculations\",\"/inference/inputs\"]},\"output\":{\"verdict\":\"PASS\",\"notes\":\"N003 states the derivative identity for every positive r, including the integral, factor 2, and wave-operator expression. S001 fixes arbitrary r and purports to derive precisely that statement; the failure is in the argument, not an identified mismatch in the output formulation.\",\"locations\":[\"/output/statement\",\"/inference/argument/0/text\"]},\"scope\":{\"verdict\":\"PASS\",\"notes\":\"The argument fixes arbitrary r>0 and invokes N001 for a time window with r in its interior. Its coordinate-index argument is explicitly intended for arbitrary j followed by summation over the finite index set. No scope escape is needed for the identified failure.\",\"locations\":[\"/inference/locals/0\",\"/inference/locals/1\",\"/inference/argument/0/text\"]},\"applicability\":{\"verdict\":\"PASS\",\"notes\":\"The M001 application specifies the Euclidean/Borel/Lebesgue space, scalar codomain, finite-on-compacts fact, and continuity/compact-support grounds. M011's application gives the vector spaces, dot-product bilinear map, integrability obligations, derivative identities, and relevant supports; M017 is applied to the finite index coordinate realization to supply the Haar-measure fact. These application checks do not prove the separate mixed-partials implication.\",\"locations\":[\"/inference/applications/0\",\"/inference/applications/1\",\"/inference/applications/2\",\"/inputs/2/source_contract\",\"/inputs/3/source_contract\",\"/inputs/4/source_contract\"]},\"statement_closure\":{\"verdict\":\"PASS\",\"notes\":\